## Finding 3, tested on copies of the emitted C (not changed in the store)

Each of these left the output byte-identical and the stack still growing (VmStk after 3 s on 2 MB):
every generated function given one arity (24) — 570 MB; that plus `musttail` on every tail call
between generated functions, statement expressions and switch arms rewritten as returns — 658 MB;
`hy_app`'s saturated-call array made thread-local so the call into the closure has no escaping local —
453 MB; both together — 379 MB. Two hops stay non-tail: the closure wrapper `wN(const uint64_t *a)`
calls `gN(a[0], …)` with up to 24 arguments from a one-register frame, and recursions on a Nat are
emitted as `if (n == 0) r = …; else r = …; return r;`. A constant stack needs the continuation call
(`hy_app` → `wN` → `gN`) to be one convention end to end: either every generated function and closure
entry takes its arguments the same way (e.g. one pointer to an argument block the callee copies
before its first call), so every tail call can be `musttail`, or continuation calls return to the
`hy_run_step` loop instead of nesting.
